/E (Objective expectation: Society must view such an expectation as fair, normal, and reasonable, especially depending on the situation.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
283 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 192 0 R
/E (Fourth Amendment: The Fourth Amendment protects people from unreasonable searches and seizures by the government. It generally requires police to have probable cause and a specific warrant before conducting a search, although there are exceptions to the warrant requirement.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
284 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 192 0 R
/E (Unreasonable: An action, choice, or belief that goes against common sense, is unfair, comes from a nonfactual basis, or falls below the standard of care expected from an ordinary person.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
285 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 192 0 R
/E (Probable cause: This requires police to have reasonable grounds, based on facts and circumstances, to believe that a crime has been committed, that a specific person committed it, or that evidence of a crime is present before making an arrest or conducting a search.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [12]
>>
