/P 194 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
325 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 194 0 R
/E (There are some extras to these rules, like everything when it comes to the law. Ownership can support an expectation of privacy, but it does not automatically create one, just as not owning something does not automatically eliminate one. For example, imagine a student keeping a personal journal. The journal belongs to them, they keep it closed, and they do not generally allow other people to read it. They would likely have a strong personal expectation that its contents remain private. But a reasonable expectation of privacy does not mean that the journal can never be searched. A staff member within a school may still be legally permitted to search something in which a person has a privacy interest if reasonable suspicion is fulfilled. The existence of a privacy interest and the legality of a search are two separate questions.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
