337 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 195 0 R
/E (Reasonable suspicion is a legal standard that allows a government official, which we are classifying as school staff and school police, to take action on a suspicion that has been created based on facts and circumstances that create a reasonable basis to suspect that an individual\(s\) has committed a wrongdoing. \(Or, in the case of random searches or suspicionless searches, any special condition or status that makes it appropriate to conduct those searches.\) For example, things that can contribute to reasonable suspicion include physical indicators, visible clear items, direct reports, etc.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
338 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 195 0 R
/E (The two standards that a suspicion needs to fit to be a sufficient, feasible suspicion to search a student are: first, the search is justified at its beginning, meaning that there are reasonable conditions for suspecting that searching a student will reveal evidence that the student has violated or is violating the law or school rules and could cause foreseeable harm.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
